WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.820
&gt;&gt;Malcolm grant: Neil, please come and join
us. Let me invite Neil Churchill to join the

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:19.140
table. Neil is our director for patient experience,
and I am going to invite him to introduce

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.400
for us the paper on implementation of the
Freedom to Speak Up recommendations on whistle-blowing.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:35.240
Neil, welcome. I apologise. Karen will set
the context, and then Neil.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:42.290
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: I am just going to remind
everyone that, as a result of a number of

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:49.720
somewhat distressing and high-profile whistle-blowing
cases in recent times, Robert Francis was

00:00:49.720 --> 00:01:00.600
invited to conduct a review of whistle-blowing.
As a result, he brought his report and recommendations

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:09.369
to address the cases and ensure that whistle-blowers
are properly heard, responded to and dealt

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:11.040
with.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:18.430
I am delighted that Dame Eileen Sills has
been appointed as the national guardian as

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:23.390
a result of those recommendations, and we
very much welcome her appointment and look

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:28.100
forward to working with her, along with the
other arm's-length bodies, in the work that

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:33.480
we in NHS England specifically need to do.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:39.979
I am taking the lead in this for NHS England
itself, so I am overseeing the implementation

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:45.310
of the recommendations for NHS England as
we become a prescribed organisation and have

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:51.540
specific responsibilities for whistle-blowers
from primary care, as well as our own staff,

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:58.440
of course. With that, I would very much like
to ask Neil to talk in more detail about the

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.330
report.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:05.780
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: Thank you. Just to remind
you of the context, and building on what Karen

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:12.349
has said, whistle blowing has a broader influence
on NHS staff attitudes. We know from the staff

00:02:12.349 --> 00:02:17.650
survey that currently round about 70% of NHS
staff feel that they would be confident in

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:23.409
raising a concern about patient safety, but
that means that 30% do not say that they would

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:28.879
be confident in raising a concern. If they
are not, then we are missing opportunities

00:02:28.879 --> 00:02:33.290
to keep people safe and give people a good
experience.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:39.989
Robert Francis also indicated that staff in
primary care are a vulnerable group; because

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:44.459
they are working in smaller organisations
it is harder to raise issues outside of the

00:02:44.459 --> 00:02:49.519
line management structure, and there are fewer
outside organisations for them to go to. That

00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:55.109
was why he recommended that NHS England become
a prescribed body for whistle-blowers, and

00:02:55.109 --> 00:03:02.099
we are on track to get ourselves into that
position by 1st April, when the regulations

00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:03.779
will start to apply to us.

00:03:03.779 --> 00:03:09.639
It is important to say that we are already
doing this work. Medical directors are already

00:03:09.639 --> 00:03:15.120
talking to NHS whistle-blowers, and we have
already seen some examples where we have responded

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.840
to whistle-blowers' concerns, but we need
to make sure that we are doing that consistently

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:25.519
and in keeping with Robert Francis's principles,
and we need to be able to pull together a

00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:29.559
central report that will tell you how on many
occasions this is happening, what kind of

00:03:29.559 --> 00:03:34.159
things we are doing and what we are learning.
It is worth saying that this is really the

00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:41.139
flip side of what we are also doing for patient
complaints. Last year the ombudsman produced

00:03:41.139 --> 00:03:47.049
a set of 'I' statements about how patient
complaints should be handled. We are implementing

00:03:47.049 --> 00:03:53.169
those within NHS England, and we are encouraging
primary care to introduce them too.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:04:00.839
On the whole, we are on track. The main risk
for us at the moment is that the reporting

00:04:00.839 --> 00:04:06.699
requirements for being a prescribed organisation
are not yet published, and so we do not know

00:04:06.699 --> 00:04:11.399
with complete confidence what information
we will need to collect from 1st April.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:17.459
We are working on the 2015 requirements, but
we are confident that we will be there and

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:19.379
ready when this applies.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:27.080
Dame Eileen's appointment is really good news.
It is really important that we have some co

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:35.509
ordinated leadership on this across the NHS.
The other ALBs are also very supportive, and

00:04:35.509 --> 00:04:41.050
we have had our first meeting with Eileen
already. It is worth saying that the CQC envisaged

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:48.690
that primary care will be excluded from her
initial remit while she focuses on NHS trusts.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:54.860
We will be having a conversation, probably
in the third quarter of the year, about when

00:04:54.860 --> 00:05:00.550
she expects to be able to get round to primary
care. The issue for me, really, is that we

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:06.800
need to have a joined-up response. As Robert
Francis has already indicated that staff in

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.860
primary care are a vulnerable group, we want
to make sure that we do not leave them out

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:15.860
and that we support Freedom to Speak Up guardians
who are working there. I am confident that

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:19.599
we will have a timetable from the CQC about
when that will happen.

00:05:19.599 --> 00:05:25.889
It is also important to say that, from the
whistle-blower community's perspective, what

00:05:25.889 --> 00:05:31.810
they are particularly looking at is the introduction
of the employment support scheme for current

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:36.379
and former NHS staff who have raised concerns
and are having difficulty gaining employment

00:05:36.379 --> 00:05:40.400
in the NHS as a result. We are making good
progress.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:49.240
It is quite a tricky area because, as we know
from Robert Francis's report, those cases

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:53.199
where concerns have been raised are often
quite complicated and there are disputed perspectives

00:05:53.199 --> 00:05:57.960
about what has happened. We have had some
very good engagement from whistle-blowers

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:02.620
and from regulators and employers, and we
are close to having an idea about how this

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.509
scheme will work.

00:06:04.509 --> 00:06:09.110
I really appreciate the urgency with which
whistle-blowers want this to come into effect

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:15.810
from April, so that is something that we are
giving a lot of time to at the moment. That

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:19.569
is what I would like to introduce, but I am
very happy to take questions and give you

00:06:19.569 --> 00:06:21.020
further detail and assurance as needed.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:27.319
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Neil. Let us open
it up for questions and comments to Neil.

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:28.000
Moira.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:37.819
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Thank you, Neil. It is the legal
regulatory section. I realise it is not absolutely

00:06:37.819 --> 00:06:45.620
certain, but I am interested in the 'required
to report'. Required to report to whom? Also,

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:48.719
what will the role of this Board be in that?

00:06:48.719 --> 00:06:57.249
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: We will need to publish
a report about the number of whistle-blowers

00:06:57.249 --> 00:07:03.789
that have raised concerns with us, what we
have done with those concerns and how many

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:10.349
we have referred on to other organisations.
It is a combination of data and narrative.

00:07:10.349 --> 00:07:15.629
The Department of Health ran a consultation
last year which suggested that there might

00:07:15.629 --> 00:07:21.460
be some flexibility about how that report
came together, and what we suggest is that

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:26.780
we will combine the whistle blowing information
with the patient complaints information, because

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:30.629
this is basically about how good we are at
listening to people and acting on what they

00:07:30.629 --> 00:07:31.219
say.

00:07:31.219 --> 00:07:35.699
There will be learning from both sides, so
we are hopeful that we will be able to produce

00:07:35.699 --> 00:07:40.120
a single combined report that covers both
of those, but we are awaiting the confirmation

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:45.960
of the regulations to make sure that that
will be possible.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:52.659
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: It is important that this Board
takes a view of that, obviously, but the culture

00:07:52.659 --> 00:07:59.419
change and the culture-setting is much more
important. Even if it is, in reporting terms,

00:07:59.419 --> 00:08:05.999
part of another report, we would want something
that, as it begins, gives us food for thought,

00:08:05.999 --> 00:08:11.090
really, and tries to ensure that we are shaping
it as well as we could.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:19.580
&gt;&gt;Margaret Casely-Hayford: I really welcome
the fact that NHS England is seen as the prescribed

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:25.969
person, because of its independence. I think
what is going to be very helpful to whistle-blowers

00:08:25.969 --> 00:08:33.690
is actually getting a response about what
actions are going to be taken. I think it

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:40.000
is necessarily a very isolated position that
they find themselves in, and so part of what

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:47.170
is important about the role of NHS England
in this process is the fact that it is a sort

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:56.700
of backstop response. It would be useful to
see what formality there is around making

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:01.090
sure that individuals feel supported.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:06.610
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: I think there are a number
of dimensions to that. We certainly do need

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:14.450
to tell people what we have done. I know that
medical directors and nursing directors who

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:18.070
are handling these calls at the moment make
every effort to do that.

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:23.750
There are often some restrictions on how much
we can say, but nevertheless I think there

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:28.280
are lessons that we can learn from other sectors
where people are already prescribed organisations.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:33.900
We are talking to the CQC, and we are looking
at the financial sector and others, to learn

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:35.380
from that.

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:39.170
In relation to the support that people get,
Robert Francis was very clear about people

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:48.730
having access to a range of things like mediation,
where appropriate. We are issuing guidance

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:56.040
for primary care about how organisations in
primary care can support people, by indicating

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:59.850
where you can go externally but also looking
at what kind of things need to be available

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:04.150
to make sure that relationships do not deteriorate
and that people do not find themselves unable

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:07.270
to continue to work in a particular practice.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:12.740
I think the nature of that and how it is provided
will vary, because you have such a diversity

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:17.410
of organisations in primary care, from quite
big ones which are well federated to very

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:24.310
small ones which are not. We are going to
have to work to build up that capacity over

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:28.080
time, but that is certainly something that
we are addressing.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:34.060
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Neil, when you introduced
the paper you talked about whistle-blowing

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:38.510
in the context of patient safety, and of course
that derives from Robert Francis's report,

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:45.560
but whistle blowing does not always fit exactly
into one particular category. It may coincide

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:50.020
with complaints about, let's say, financial
irregularities in an organisation. How do

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:56.600
you see this working across the broad array
of whistle-blowing instances in primary care

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:58.190
and within this organisation?

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:05.260
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: We are trying to make it
simple. As you say, there are a number of

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:12.330
different dimensions to it. There are different
places where you would go to report fraud

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:18.900
or a concern about patient safety, or a concern
that services are not good enough to meet

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:25.050
the needs of patients or carers. I think you
are right; there are a number of initiatives

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:30.420
at the moment which are a little bit of a
patchwork. You have the whistle-blowing work,

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:34.400
the patient complaints work and some recent
work that ministers have started on bullying.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:40.070
We have learning organisations. There is a
challenge to all of us about how we tie these

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:45.730
together, and there is a strong appetite from
NHS organisations to tell a simple, joined-up

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:51.800
story and make it really clear to people what
they need to do. I do not think we have succeeded

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:58.470
yet in doing that at a national level, but
it is certainly on the agenda. Basically this

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:03.510
is about how you listen to people, how you
act on what they tell you and how you report

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:08.920
back on what you have done as a result. We
need to do that consistently. There is more

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:11.060
we need to do to get this right.

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:16.720
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Simplifying the channel and
the advice and support for those who wish

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:19.160
to speak up is a critical part of this.

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:22.570
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: Yes. Just on that point,
we want to make sure there is no wrong door

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:27.180
at NHS England. At the end of the day, the
people who will be dealing with whistle-blowers

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:31.580
will be very experienced people who have done
this for some time. Whistle-blowers, as we

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:36.650
know, can contact us in a number of different
ways: by email, by telephone, through the

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:40.680
contact centre or locally. We need to make
sure that there is no wrong door, but that

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:42.190
our response is consistently good.

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:49.430
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I just want to support
the point that Moira made. I think this is

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:53.340
quite a challenge, actually; quite a significant
challenge. You can have as much structure

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:57.900
as you like, but it is culture that will make
the difference, regardless of how many doors

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:05.680
there are. I suppose the question I have is:
what OD support is there for actually supporting

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:10.730
the culture shift, and what part does the
Board have in keeping that live? Otherwise,

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:17.610
we will just have a set of dials but no way
to move the needle.

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:30.040
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: Clearly I am here for NHS
England, and it is even more challenging for

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:36.700
primary care to answer that question. I cannot
really answer for primary care, but we will

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:43.160
certainly be discussing these sorts of issues
around the Board that oversees this work with

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:47.790
Ros Roughton's representative, David Geddes,
who works on primary care, to make sure that

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:55.040
we tackle those. For NHS England, of course,
we see this as sitting alongside our work

00:13:55.040 --> 00:14:02.750
on bullying and harassment and other types
of interventions around staff engagement and

00:14:02.750 --> 00:14:11.620
management skills more generally. It absolutely
needs to be connected with those areas of

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:16.670
work, to address the underlying culture issues.
As we have discussed, Victor, I am very keen

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:17.360
to do that.

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:20.950
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Are we working in partnership
with the Workforce Race Equality Standard?

00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:22.590
Because there are some obvious pointers.

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:27.860
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: Yes, absolutely. Part of
our engagement work within NHS England absolutely

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:32.280
addresses diversity and the work that we need
to do around that, so it is part and parcel

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:35.140
of the same piece of work.

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:40.230
&gt;&gt;Margaret Casely-Hayford: Can I come back
on the point that you made, Chairman? I think

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:49.470
it is absolutely critical that the simplicity
of the structure is made public. Some sort

00:14:49.470 --> 00:14:58.220
of ownership chart for the various strands
- who owns the forward complaints, who owns

00:14:58.220 --> 00:15:07.120
the clinical within a specific sphere, the
HR complaints and so on - so that it is very

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:15.070
clear that there is actually a structure that
one can see. I completely recognise that it

00:15:15.070 --> 00:15:26.590
is much more a cultural than a physical paper
OD issue, but I think the Chairman's point

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:36.180
about seeing something that recognises that,
albeit there is no wrong door, there are specific

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:44.050
channels so that certain types of complaints
land in specific places, is really helpful

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:46.510
to everybody within the system.

00:15:46.510 --> 00:15:53.670
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: Yes. We have that thrust,
and I can share that with you. The other thing

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:57.690
to say is we are working very closely with
Public Concern at Work to get our policies

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:02.410
right and our communications right, and they
are going to be coming in to help with some

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:06.890
briefing and some training for the people
working on this. They have a lot of experience

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:12.590
in other sectors, as well as from the health
sector, so we will be there.

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:17.670
I think the wider system is a bit more complicated,
because we will be prescribed for primary

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:23.630
care and not for NHS trusts. I think that
is right, but I expect that we will have quite

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:28.550
a few people come to us who we will then signpost
to other organisations. We will be able to

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:33.020
tell you how many people we are doing that
for in the data that we provide to you.

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:35.230
&gt;&gt;Margaret Casely-Hayford: Thank you.

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:41.690
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: More an observation, really.
All of this is critical - senior ownership

00:16:41.690 --> 00:16:48.180
and your own responsibility, Malcolm - in
overseeing this for the Board. The fact that

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:56.000
people know that this is taken seriously is
all-important, but we must not forget that

00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:00.230
we should really be encouraging our people
to feel that they have the confidence to be

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:06.189
able to talk to their line managers about
concerns. Therefore, alongside this, Karen,

00:17:06.189 --> 00:17:16.550
I hope that we are having support and interventions
in place to help line managers understand

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:19.490
that this can be some of the most valuable
feedback that they get, and it should not

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:28.120
be seen as critical. Also, we should encourage
people to first and foremost raise their concerns,

00:17:28.120 --> 00:17:34.800
with their immediate line manager and, indeed,
up that line, with this as a very important

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:38.840
safety net when people feel that does not
work.

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:47.800
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: David, you are absolutely
right. That is why I do see this as needing

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:52.430
to be looked at alongside our own work on
bullying, harassment and management skills.

00:17:52.430 --> 00:17:58.170
This is part of what managers should be doing
with their staff, and creating the culture

00:17:58.170 --> 00:18:06.150
in their teams for people to raise concerns
in an effective way. I completely agree with

00:18:06.150 --> 00:18:07.770
you; that is some of the work that we are
doing already.

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:16.670
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: We should have a pretty low
tolerance for behaviour that is symptomatic

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:21.750
of bullying and harassment. I think it is
important that there are mechanisms in place,

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:27.100
through the engagement survey, through the
barometer and through our own understanding,

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:33.460
to be clear about what we expect and also
what we will not tolerate. I think that nothing

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:35.800
sends signals better than taking action.

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:41.720
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: Yes, I completely agree with
you. I think we need to do, as part of the

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:50.160
implementation for the whistle-blowing work
in particular, some quite considerable communications

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:56.150
around our organisation. That is our real
opportunity to make it really clear what we

00:18:56.150 --> 00:19:02.940
expect of staff and managers as we go to implement
this work.

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:11.420
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Also for people to see conversations
about people's concerns as being a real opportunity

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:18.810
to learn and develop and improve the environment,
not as a threat to themselves, is at the core

00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:19.180
of it.

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:19.900
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: Yes, absolutely.

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:25.530
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: That is so important, culturally,
for ourselves. It is around primary care that

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:32.020
the culture change that we can help to lead
from here is probably more challenging, working

00:19:32.020 --> 00:19:35.960
with partners to be able to bring that about.
However, I think the existence of a clear

00:19:35.960 --> 00:19:42.570
channel for speaking up through to us at this
Board across primary care is going to be a

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:44.280
very important first step.

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:50.860
Are there any other questions or comments
on this? If not, may I thank Karen and Neil.

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:52.050
Thank you very much for joining us this morning.

